From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Diljit Singh <hey@djdev.me>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 07:40:26 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] drm/apple: stop and restart the T8140 display processors for
 a deep sleep

A deep sleep powers both display coprocessors off, and the sleep engine
waits on DISP_CPU with no timeout, so the firmware has to be stopped
through its driver and its domains lowered before the commit. Each step
below is what macOS does, measured under aura:

- The internal DCP's stop: set_power_state(0) in band after A414(0),
  A478 x2, A476(1), A478, A412; D572(0) from the firmware turns the
  display DART's streams off; A500; RTKit idle with the secure RTBuddy
  port answered between AP and IOP idle; the idle and stopped status
  bits; the mailbox off and the run bit cleared twice; the DCP DART's
  streams off with its GAPF slice released. A refused idle is retried
  once after draining what's queued. If it's refused again the firmware
  keeps running with the display off, and DPMS on lights it there; the
  run bit is never cleared under running firmware.
- The external DCP's stop: A500, then about 4 ms later AP and IOP idle
  and the run bit, through the shared RTKit stop. An attached display
  refuses the sleep, since nothing brings it back yet.
- The secure RTBuddy port. The DCP's firmware takes IOP idle only with
  its secure port answered, so the driver answers its HELLO and endpoint
  map at bind and again after a warm restart, between the main port's
  IOP report and its AP on. Its interrupts aren't in the DT, so the
  rest is polled: every jiffy for a second after its start or a
  message, otherwise every 100 ms, as a CPU woken every jiffy never
  reaches deep idle.
- OSLOG counters. A DCP restarted warm holds each log counter until the
  last is echoed and refuses its next AP idle with one outstanding, so
  both DCPs have them echoed, as macOS does.
- Display on after a sleep: the domains back up in macOS's order, the
  DART protected again, the warm restart, then the named calls and
  set_power_state(1) out of band. D572(1) turns the scanout DART back
  on. If that fails the panel stays dark until reboot and the DCP is
  treated as crashed.

DPMS on the internal panel now does the same power cycle as macOS's
display sleep: the backlight to 0, the firmware stopped and the display
domains off, and the reverse on wake. Not during a system sleep, which
stops the firmware itself. While the display is off its DART's registers
aren't touched.

apple-drm doesn't refuse a suspend whose display firmware is already
stopped, a commit that leaves a crashed DCP's CRTC off passes, and DP
audio refuses a sleep with a stream open. No mode fits a crashed DCP, so
after a refused sleep that left one stopped the resume restores its CRTC
disabled and userspace sets it again on the hotplug, rather than the
commit failing with -22 and every output staying dark.

A DPMS power-on that fails after the warm restart has raised the display
domains and set the run bit leaves the firmware running while the driver
takes it as stopped; the next sleep would skip the stop, find a bound
display coprocessor running, and be refused, as every later one. On a
J700 a power-on cut short as a sleep began did this at the 27th sleep in
a row. So the driver tracks whether it raised the domains since it last
lowered them, and clears the run bit of a DCP taken as stopped that has
them up and running before the sleep, whose warm restart then brings it
back.

Only T8140 with H17P firmware takes these paths.

Signed-off-by: Diljit Singh <hey@djdev.me>

[j604 backport: resolved against Aurora code this branch does not carry (T6041 takeover abort, H17G panel blank, panel metadata helpers; A428 is dcpep_pipe_set_428 here): only this commit's own lines kept. The crashed check in dcp_crtc_mode_fixup() moved after the M4 branch, since M4 sets crashed itself (iomfb_m4.c l.370); no t8132_ext_drm.c here (dcpext_drm.c has its own drm_device, so the t8132_ext_crtc() test is dropped) and no DCP_FW_DESKTOP lineage (DCP_FW_H17() written as the template's DCP_FW_VER >= 26.0, true only for iomfb_h17p.c). Every new path is gated on H17P firmware + apple,t8140-dcp(ext) (dcp_s2r_capable/_ext_capable, dcp_dpms_power_cycles) or on pm_suspend_via_firmware(), which only apple-s2r sets: inert on T8132 and under s2idle. The T8140 sequence values are Aurora's measurements; none were traced here]
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5.5

